      "math_academy_id": "q-43659",
      "knowledge_point_id": "46e1513d-0944-5c09-8a7e-e1d9ae81bae5",
      "knowledge_point": "Graphing the Inverse Tangent Function",
      "problem": "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n\n- There is a zero at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty ,0)$ and positive over $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n\n- $y\\to -\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from above) as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $y\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from below) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The lines $y=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)",
