      "knowledge_point": "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function",
      "problem": "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(-\\sqrt{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n\nII. $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n\nIII. $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$",
      "worked_solution": "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(-\\sqrt{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\sqrt{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$",
